## What the audit log shows

The extra frontend servers don't come from deployments alone. Seven times in about 15 hours the group grew from 1 to 3 with no build running, then shrank back to 1 on its own.

| Scale-out (UTC) | Nearest deployment | Back to 1 server |
|---|---|---|
| Oct 4 17:55 | none within 40 min | 18:12 |
| Oct 4 20:11 | none within 55 min | 20:25 |
| Oct 4 21:24 | none within 40 min | 21:40 |
| Oct 5 03:22 | none within hours | 03:35 |
| Oct 5 04:22 | none | 04:36 |
| Oct 5 05:24 | none within 15 min | 05:44 |
| Oct 5 08:09 | none within 25 min | 08:41 |

- **Two servers are created within ~30 to 50 seconds each time.** That's one autoscaler step, and the shrink follows 13 to 17 minutes later, which is consistent with the 600-second scale-in wait. The one exception is 08:09, which took 32 minutes.
- **Deployments are noisy too.** The 06:29 build produced five server creations and five deletions over 25 minutes, ending with one server. The 07:26 build was followed by two extra servers at 07:43, about 9 minutes into the rollout. This fits my earlier guess that boot-time CPU counts as load. That guess is still unproven.
- **Cost:** roughly 4 extra `e2-medium` server-hours in 15 hours from the pairs alone, plus the deployment churn. It's small but recurring.

The standalone pairs need a different explanation, because no server was booting at those times. What pushes an idle server's CPU above 60% from time to time, I can't tell from these outputs.

## Two read-only checks for the cause

**1. Cloud Shell. The project has a Web Security Scanner.** Its log was in your earlier list, and a crawler hitting the site could cause these spikes. This lists its activity times to compare with the seven times above:
```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609
gcloud logging read 'LOG_ID("websecurityscanner.googleapis.com/scan_activity")' --project=$P --freshness=24h --limit=40 --format="value(timestamp)"
```

**2. From the backend server (not Cloud Shell, which isn't inside the VPC and can't reach internal IPs). Look at what ran on the current frontend server around the 08:09 spike.** If gcloud says reauthentication is needed, run `gcloud auth login --no-launch-browser` first.
```bash
P=pg-ae-n-app-125609
N=$(gcloud compute instances list --project=$P --filter="name~cn-frontend-mig" --format="value(name)" --limit=1)
Z=$(gcloud compute instances list --project=$P --filter="name~cn-frontend-mig" --format="value(zone.basename())" --limit=1)
gcloud compute ssh $N --zone=$Z --project=$P --internal-ip --command="sudo journalctl --since \"2026-10-05 08:05:00\" --until \"2026-10-05 08:11:00\" --no-pager | grep -v GoogleHC | cut -c1-170 | tail -40"
gcloud compute ssh $N --zone=$Z --project=$P --internal-ip --command="sudo grep -E '05/Oct/2026:08:0[5-9]|05/Oct/2026:08:10' /var/log/apache2/careernavigator-frontend-staging-access.log | grep -v GoogleHC | sed 's/.*\" \"//;s/\"\$//' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -8"
```
The second command prints only client identifiers (user agents) and counts, not paths. Scan both outputs before pasting, because the journal may contain account names.

The Console is also useful: Compute Engine → Instance groups → `cn-frontend-mig-regional` → Monitoring, with a screenshot of CPU and group size for the last 12 hours.

## Fix options, nothing applied yet

| | What | Cost | Risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| **A. Lengthen the cool-down** | autoscaler cool-down 60s to 300s | none | smaller chance that boot CPU triggers more servers. It won't stop the idle-time spikes |
| **B. Cap max at 2** | max replicas 3 to 2 | lower ceiling | less headroom for real load |
| **C. Observe** | leave as is | up to 2 extra servers for 15 to 30 min each time | none |

**Recommendation: A first**, then check the next two deployments. B is the safety net if the 3-server pairs continue. Console: Compute Engine → Instance groups → `cn-frontend-mig-regional` → Edit group → Autoscaling → Cool-down period `300` → Save. Rollback is setting it back to 60. It only changes future scaling decisions.

## Backend is growing too

The backend group is at size 3 with target 1, so it's shrinking from a scale-out. Check its autoscaler settings, which I never saw, and its recent creations:
```bash
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $(gcloud auth print-access-token)" "https://compute.googleapis.com/compute/v1/projects/$P/regions/me-central2/autoscalers/cn-backend-mig-regional-np3m" | grep -E "minNumReplicas|maxNumReplicas|coolDownPeriodSec|stabilizationPeriodSec|recommendedSize|status"
gcloud logging read 'logName:"cloudaudit.googleapis.com%2Factivity" AND protoPayload.methodName="v1.compute.instances.insert" AND protoPayload.resourceName:"cn-backend-mig-regional"' --project=$P --freshness=24h --format="value(timestamp,protoPayload.resourceName.basename())" | sort -r | head -30
```
Don't push to the backend until it's back at 1. A rollout with `--max-surge=3` stacks on top of the extra servers.

